<header><p class="st-kicker">republic · Horn and East Africa</p><h1 class="st-title">Republic of Burundi</h1><p class="st-meta">1966 CE – present · capital Gitega</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The republic that replaced Burundi's monarchy in 1966, marked by military rule, mass killings and civil war before a fragile peace from 2000.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>In November 1966 Captain <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/michel-micombero">Michel Micombero</a> deposed the young king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ntare-v-of-burundi">Ntare V</a>, ending the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/kingdom-of-burundi">Kingdom of Burundi</a> and proclaiming a republic. Power rested with a Tutsi-dominated army and the single party <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/union-for-national-progress">UPRONA</a>, founded by the independence hero <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-rwagasore">Prince Louis Rwagasore</a>, who had been assassinated in 1961.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>After a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hutu">Hutu</a> rebellion in 1972 the army killed perhaps 100,000 to 200,000 Hutu, mainly the educated, in the killings known as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ikiza">Ikiza</a>. Further coups brought <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jean-baptiste-bagaza">Jean-Baptiste Bagaza</a> in 1976 and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pierre-buyoya">Pierre Buyoya</a> in 1987. In 1993 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/melchior-ndadaye">Melchior Ndadaye</a> became the first elected Hutu president, but his assassination months later started a civil war that killed some 300,000 people.</p><h2>To the present</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arusha-peace-and-reconciliation-agreement-for-burundi">Arusha Accords</a> of 2000 and a 2005 constitution introduced ethnic power-sharing, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pierre-nkurunziza">Pierre Nkurunziza</a> ruled from 2005 to 2020, his disputed third term sparking a crisis in 2015. The political capital moved to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gitega">Gitega</a> in 2019, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/evariste-ndayishimiye">Evariste Ndayishimiye</a> succeeded Nkurunziza in 2020.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/republic-of-burundi.jpg" alt="Republic of Burundi" width="320" height="192"><figcaption>Image: See File history below for details. · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Burundi.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1966 CE – present</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/21st-century">21st century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>republic</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>bureaucratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Gitega</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Michel Micombero, Pierre Buyoya, Pierre Nkurunziza</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Kirundi, French, English</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/burundi">Burundi</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Burundi (republic)</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1996&amp;focus=republic-of-burundi" aria-label="Republic of Burundi on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5511 2306 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Republic of Burundi lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5831 2562L5827 2571"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5827 2571h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5831 2562h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5831 2562h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Gitega. <a href="/atlas?year=1996&amp;focus=republic-of-burundi">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
